BT/F1 12 Tf318.5 45 TD 0 Tc 0 Tw (25)Tj/F3 14 Tf-210 663 TD 1.167 Tc (13.)Tj21 0 TD ( )Tj7 0 TD 0.392 Tc (CONCLUSIONS)Tj/F1 12 Tf8 -20 TD -0.131 Tc (While many jurisdictions do not actually consider their choice of revenue)Tj-36 -18 TD -0.043 Tc (mechanism, they probably should.  Changes in technology and funding sources suggest)Tj0 -18 TD -0.089 Tc (that a reconsideration of the standard financing mechanisms is warranted. The purpose of)TjT* -0.105 Tc (this research was to develop a model to explain whether jurisdictions choose to tax or to toll)TjT* -0.074 Tc (as a function of the length of trips, the size of the jurisdiction, the transactions costs of)TjT* -0.076 Tc (collecting revenue, demand, and the cost of providing infrastructure.  In short the intent)TjT* -0.096 Tc (was to help frame the analysis of how jurisdictions have historically considered, albeit in a)TjT* -0.115 Tc (more complex and less mathematical environment, and may in the future consider, the)TjT* -0.105 Tc (choice of revenue mechanism.)Tj36 -18 TD -0.13 Tc (Large jurisdictions are more likely to impose taxes or a mixed financing policy than)Tj-36 -18 TD -0.077 Tc (only cordon tolls because cordon tolls raise insufficient revenues to cover costs, as revenue)Tj0 -18 TD -0.093 Tc (levels off above a certain point.   Similarly, the higher the cost of collecting tolls, the less)TjT* -0.135 Tc (likely tolls will be the preferred revenue mechanism.  The welfare maximizing toll may not)TjT* -0.079 Tc (fully recover costs, and still require subsidy.  The maximum welfare from taxes may)TjT* -0.097 Tc (exceed those of tolls under certain circumstances, depending on model parameters,)TjT* -0.101 Tc (however if jurisdictions are sufficiently small, demand sufficiently higher, and collection)TjT* -0.071 Tc (costs relatively low, then tolls will be preferred.)Tj36 -18 TD -0.099 Tc (The gains to a jurisdiction of imposing tolls exceeds the gains from taxes under)Tj-36 -18 TD -0.082 Tc (certain circumstances.  The gains come foremost from residents of other jurisdictions.  This)Tj0 -18 TD -0.092 Tc (problem, a finance externality, is well known in certain cases, for instance the reliance by)TjT* -0.065 Tc (local governments on some mix of sales, income, and property taxes, each of which are)TjT* -0.074 Tc (borne by a different set of people, not all of whom are local.)Tj36 -18 TD -0.076 Tc (Congestion pricing has long been a goal of transport economists, who argue that it)Tj-36 -18 TD -0.089 Tc (will result in more efficient use of resources.  The path for implementing such a system has)Tj0 -18 TD -0.083 Tc (been strewn with political potholes, pricing inevitably creates winners and losers.  An)TjT* -0.12 Tc (alternative approach, one which would create the local winners necessary to implement)TjT* -0.088 Tc (road pricing, is required before congestion pricing can be expected to become widespread.)TjT* -0.095 Tc (This research suggests one approach, one that would decentralize the decision about)TjT* -0.099 Tc (whether to tax or toll before attempting to impose road pricing.  Road pricing is a necessary)TjT* -0.095 Tc (prerequisite to congestion pricing, which once tolls are in place, is not nearly as difficult)TjT* -0.112 Tc (problem as placing tolls on untolled roads in the first place.  And tolls are a rational)TjT* -0.121 Tc (financing mechanism for a sufficiently small jurisdiction, particularly with the advent of)TjT* -0.132 Tc (electronic toll collection systems.)Tj36 -18 TD -0.063 Tc (The prospects for future success of toll roads depend on several factors, including)Tj-36 -18 TD -0.113 Tc (the relative centralization of control of the highway sector, and the transaction costs of)TjETendstreamendobj88 0 obj3761endobj85 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 86 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F1 6 0 R /F3 8 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 87 0 R>>endobj90 0 obj<</Length 91 0 R>>stream
